<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Marshall</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1755–1835</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Marshall was the fourth chief justice of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, serving from 1801 to 1835, the longest tenure in the court's history. In decisions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marbury-v-madison">Marbury v. Madison</a> (1803), which established judicial review, and McCulloch v. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> (1819), he strengthened the Supreme Court and the federal government; he had earlier served as secretary of state under John Adams.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-marshall.jpg" alt="John Marshall" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Inman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Marshall_by_Henry_Inman,_1832.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
